/// AgentforceSalesQuestionBank — 126 questions (104 single-select, 22 multi-select)
import Foundation

enum AgentforceSalesQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A VP of Sales at a manufacturing distributor wants a single view showing each rep's quota attainment, pipeline coverage ratio, and weekly activity counts, refreshed daily for a Monday leadership review. Which approach should the consultant recommend?", options: [("A", "Build a dashboard combining an Opportunity report (pipeline coverage), a Forecast-based report (quota attainment), and a Task/Event activity report, refreshed on a schedule"), ("B", "Export opportunity and activity data weekly to a spreadsheet and email it to leadership manually"), ("C", "Create a single report type joining Opportunities, Tasks, and Forecasts into one flat report"), ("D", "Enable Einstein Opportunity Scoring and rely on its score as a proxy for all three metrics")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Salesforce dashboards let a consultant combine multiple report sources (opportunity, forecast, activity) into one composite view with a refresh schedule, matching the requirement for a single multi-metric snapshot without custom development. B is incorrect: Manual export defeats the requirement for a live, recurring view and introduces avoidable data-entry lag. C is incorrect: Opportunities, Tasks, and Forecasts don't share a common report type join path in native reporting, so this isn't buildable declaratively. D is incorrect: Opportunity Scoring predicts close likelihood only; it doesn't measure quota attainment or activity volume."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "A sales operations manager wants sales managers to see win rate, average deal size, and sales cycle length trending over the last four quarters without engineering support. What should the consultant recommend?", options: [("A", "A custom Apex batch job that recalculates the three metrics nightly into custom objects"), ("B", "Historical Trend Reporting combined with joined reports on closed Opportunities"), ("C", "Einstein Forecasting to project the three metrics forward"), ("D", "Person Account record types to separate the metrics by segment")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Historical Trend Reporting snapshots key opportunity fields over time, and joined reports let win rate, deal size, and cycle length appear side by side across quarters — all declarative. A is incorrect: A nightly Apex job is unnecessary custom engineering when native trend reporting already snapshots the needed history. C is incorrect: Einstein Forecasting predicts future pipeline value, not historical trend metrics like cycle length. D is incorrect: Person Accounts govern account/contact modeling for B2C selling and have no bearing on trend reporting."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "A sales director wants to see, in real time, average deal size and win rate broken out by product family for the current fiscal year. Which combination should a consultant configure?", options: [("A", "A Campaign Influence report grouped by product"), ("B", "A static list view sorted by Amount"), ("C", "An Opportunity report grouped by Product Family with summary formulas for average amount and win-rate calculation"), ("D", "A Path field-set on the Opportunity page layout")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Grouping a standard opportunity report by product family and adding summary formula fields is the native way to surface calculated, real-time KPIs segmented by product. A is incorrect: Campaign Influence reports track marketing attribution, not product-level deal size or win rate. B is incorrect: A static list view can't compute grouped averages or win-rate calculations. D is incorrect: Path is a UI guidance feature on the record, not a reporting mechanism."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "A company wants to compare the count of new opportunities created against opportunities closed each quarter to gauge pipeline health. Which tool is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Einstein Lead Scoring"), ("B", "A Path component on the Opportunity object"), ("C", "Territory Model state (Active vs. Planning)"), ("D", "A joined report combining an opportunity-created-date report block with a closed-opportunity report block, visualized on a dashboard")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A joined report lets both metrics (created vs. closed counts) sit side by side per quarter, and a dashboard chart visualizes the comparison for pipeline-health tracking. A is incorrect: Lead Scoring ranks leads by conversion likelihood; it has no relationship to opportunity creation/closure counts. B is incorrect: Path only guides a rep through stages on one record; it produces no aggregate metric. C is incorrect: Territory Model state governs territory management lifecycle, unrelated to pipeline volume comparison."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "A VP of Sales wants to identify which open deals are stalling so reps can intervene before the quarter closes. Which feature best supports this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Opportunity Scoring combined with a report filtered on days-since-last-activity"), ("B", "A report filtered on Stage Duration where time-in-stage exceeds the average for that stage"), ("C", "Lead assignment rules"), ("D", "Advanced Currency Management")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Opportunity Scoring flags declining likelihood-to-close, and pairing it with a stagnation filter (days since last activity) pinpoints exactly which deals are stalling so reps can act. C is incorrect: Assignment rules route new leads; they don't diagnose existing opportunity health. D is incorrect: Currency management handles exchange-rate conversion and has nothing to do with deal stagnation."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "Sales reps report spending significant time manually logging notes and next steps after every customer call. Which Sales Cloud capability most directly reduces this burden?", options: [("A", "Opportunity Splits"), ("B", "Einstein Conversation Insights / Sales Call Summary with auto-generated notes and follow-up tasks"), ("C", "Territory hierarchies"), ("D", "Duplicate Rules on the Contact object")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Generative call-summary capabilities automatically capture discussion points and recommended next steps, directly eliminating the manual note-taking burden the reps describe. A is incorrect: Opportunity Splits divide credit among team members; they don't touch call documentation. C is incorrect: Territory hierarchies affect account/opportunity visibility, not activity logging effort. D is incorrect: Duplicate Rules prevent redundant Contact records; unrelated to call note-taking."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "A company runs multi-touch marketing campaigns that qualify leads through a scoring threshold before handoff to sales. What should the consultant anticipate when mapping this in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "Every campaign member automatically becomes an Opportunity regardless of score"), ("B", "Lead conversion mapping is irrelevant to this process"), ("C", "Lead scoring and qualification criteria must be reflected in Lead Status values and an assignment mechanism that only triggers once the threshold is met"), ("D", "Marketing qualification can only be modeled using Person Accounts")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "The hand-off process depends on Lead Status accurately reflecting qualification stage and an assignment trigger (rule, flow) gated on the scoring threshold — this is the core configuration bridge between marketing and sales. A is incorrect: Automatic opportunity creation for every campaign member ignores the qualification gate entirely, flooding sales with unqualified leads. B is incorrect: Conversion field mapping directly determines what data sales sees post-handoff, so it is highly relevant. D is incorrect: Person Accounts model B2C individuals as accounts; they have no special role in lead scoring workflows."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "What are key considerations when integrating a marketing automation platform with Salesforce for lead handoff?", options: [("A", "Only API version compatibility matters"), ("B", "Marketing automation platforms cannot pass campaign data into Salesforce"), ("C", "Handoff should occur only through manual CSV export"), ("D", "Field mapping alignment, duplicate prevention, and a clear system-of-record for lead status between the two platforms")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Successful integrations require agreement on field mapping, deduplication logic, and which system owns lead status at each stage to avoid conflicting updates. A is incorrect: API compatibility is one technical factor but insufficient alone — governance of data ownership matters just as much. B is incorrect: Most marketing platforms integrate via native connectors or APIs that do pass campaign membership data. C is incorrect: Manual CSV export is a legacy fallback, not a recommended integration considerations for ongoing lead flow."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A company wants only leads meeting a minimum score threshold to convert into opportunities. Which mechanism best enforces this rule?", options: [("A", "A validation rule or Flow that blocks/gates lead conversion until the Lead Score field meets the threshold"), ("B", "A page layout that hides the Convert button"), ("C", "A sharing rule restricting lead visibility"), ("D", "A duplicate rule")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A validation rule or Flow-based gate directly evaluates the Lead Score field at the moment of conversion and blocks the action if the threshold isn't met — this enforces the business rule declaratively. B is incorrect: Hiding the button via layout is easily bypassed (e.g., via API or list view) and isn't a real enforcement mechanism. C is incorrect: Sharing rules control record visibility, not field-based business logic at conversion time. D is incorrect: Duplicate rules prevent redundant records; they don't evaluate score thresholds."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "An inside sales team handles inbound leads while a field sales team manages strategic accounts, both in one org. What should the consultant anticipate when configuring this?", options: [("A", "A single universal page layout for both teams to reduce complexity"), ("B", "Separate record types, page layouts, and possibly sales processes for each team, tied together through profile- or permission-set-based assignment"), ("C", "Two separate Salesforce orgs are required"), ("D", "Enterprise Territory Management to formally separate strategic named accounts from inbound-sourced accounts if territory-based rules are also needed")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Differentiated record types and page layouts let each team see fields and processes relevant to their motion, while permission sets/profiles control assignment and access — all within a single org. A is incorrect: A single layout for both teams reintroduces the very complexity the differentiated business processes require. C is incorrect: A single Salesforce org can support both motions; separate orgs aren't required."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "A company has both a fast transactional sales cycle (days) and a strategic sales cycle (months) it wants to model in Salesforce. What should guide the consultant's opportunity stage design?", options: [("A", "One universal set of stages shared by both cycles to simplify reporting"), ("B", "Removing Probability from the transactional cycle's stages"), ("C", "Two separate sales processes with distinct stage sets, each tied to its own record type"), ("D", "Disabling Opportunity Stage history tracking for the strategic cycle")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Distinct sales processes (and record types) let each motion have stages appropriate to its length and rigor, while still rolling up into unified reporting through the shared Opportunity object. A is incorrect: One shared stage set forces one cycle's stages to be meaningless for the other (e.g. a 6-stage enterprise process doesn't fit a same-day transactional deal). B is incorrect: Removing Probability breaks forecast category mapping and weighted pipeline reporting for that process. D is incorrect: Disabling stage history tracking removes velocity reporting the strategic cycle specifically needs."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "A company runs account-based marketing (ABM) campaigns and wants to tie marketing activity directly to revenue outcomes. Which feature set supports this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Lead Scoring alone"), ("B", "Opportunity Teams"), ("C", "Web-to-Lead forms only"), ("D", "Campaign Influence with multiple attribution models, rolled up through Campaign Hierarchies to closed-won Opportunity revenue")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Campaign Influence links campaigns to specific opportunities and can attribute revenue across multiple touches, and Campaign Hierarchies roll child-campaign influence up to the parent ABM program — directly connecting marketing spend to revenue. A is incorrect: Lead Scoring predicts conversion likelihood on leads; it doesn't attribute closed revenue to campaigns. B is incorrect: Opportunity Teams manage internal collaboration/credit-splitting, not marketing attribution. C is incorrect: Web-to-Lead only captures inbound form submissions; it doesn't measure ABM campaign-to-revenue linkage."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "What is the key functional difference between Collaborative Forecasting and Territory-based Forecasting?", options: [("A", "Collaborative Forecasting rolls up by role hierarchy (and optionally product/territory), while Territory-based Forecasting rolls up specifically through the Enterprise Territory Management hierarchy"), ("B", "Collaborative Forecasting only supports quantity, never revenue"), ("C", "Territory-based Forecasting cannot include quota data"), ("D", "They are the same feature with different names")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Territory-based Forecasting is a variant of Collaborative Forecasting that rolls up specifically along territory hierarchy rather than (or in addition to) role hierarchy, which matters when territory assignment—not the role tree—reflects how a company organizes revenue accountability. B is incorrect: Collaborative Forecasting supports both revenue and quantity forecast types. C is incorrect: Territory-based Forecasting fully supports quota assignment per territory. D is incorrect: They roll up along fundamentally different hierarchies and require different setup paths."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A large enterprise has hundreds of overlapping sales territories defined by geography, product line, and vertical. Which feature is purpose-built for this complexity?", options: [("A", "Public Groups with manual membership"), ("B", "Enterprise Territory Management"), ("C", "Campaign Hierarchies"), ("D", "Opportunity Teams")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Enterprise Territory Management is designed to model multi-dimensional, overlapping territory structures with rule-based account/opportunity assignment, exactly matching this scale of complexity. A is incorrect: Manually managed public groups don't scale to hundreds of overlapping, rule-driven territories. C is incorrect: Campaign Hierarchies organize marketing campaigns, not sales territory assignment. D is incorrect: Opportunity Teams add collaborators to individual deals; they don't define territory structure."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "A company wants to automate SDR outreach cadences with templated emails and call tasks at defined intervals. Which product best supports this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Activity Capture paired with Sales Engagement to auto-log the emails/calls each cadence step generates"), ("B", "Enterprise Territory Management"), ("C", "Sales Engagement (Cadences)"), ("D", "Advanced Currency Management")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Sales Engagement Cadences are purpose-built to sequence multi-step outreach (emails, calls, tasks) at defined intervals, which is exactly the SDR use case described. B is incorrect: Territory Management governs account/opportunity assignment, unrelated to outreach sequencing. D is incorrect: Currency management is a data configuration feature, irrelevant to outreach cadences."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "When should a consultant recommend Sales Engagement over a standard Salesforce task-based workflow for outreach?", options: [("A", "When only one rep sends fewer than five emails per month"), ("B", "When the company wants to eliminate all Task records"), ("C", "When the goal is purely internal team collaboration"), ("D", "When reps need structured, repeatable multi-step cadences with built-in engagement tracking (opens, replies) across a high volume of prospects")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Sales Engagement's value is in orchestrating and tracking high-volume, repeatable outreach sequences with analytics — a scale and structure that ad hoc tasks can't deliver. A is incorrect: At very low volume, the overhead of cadence setup outweighs the benefit; standard tasks suffice. B is incorrect: Cadences still generate Task records under the hood; the feature doesn't eliminate them. C is incorrect: Internal collaboration is better served by Chatter or Opportunity Teams, not outreach cadences."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A company wants reps' Outlook emails and calendar events logged into Salesforce automatically without switching applications. Which product addresses this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Activity Capture"), ("B", "Sales Engagement Cadences"), ("C", "Enterprise Territory Management"), ("D", "Opportunity Splits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Einstein Activity Capture is purpose-built to sync email and calendar activity between Outlook/Gmail and Salesforce automatically, matching this exact requirement. B is incorrect: Cadences orchestrate outbound sequences; they don't passively capture existing inbox activity. C is incorrect: Territory Management is unrelated to email/calendar sync. D is incorrect: Opportunity Splits divide revenue credit; unrelated to activity logging."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "A company enables Einstein Activity Capture to log rep activities. What is a key limitation the consultant should communicate?", options: [("A", "It cannot connect to Outlook at all"), ("B", "Captured emails/events are stored outside standard Activity records by default and have reduced reporting and workflow-automation capability compared to native Tasks/Events"), ("C", "It requires a separate Salesforce org"), ("D", "It replaces the need for a CRM entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "By default, Einstein Activity Capture stores captured activities outside the core Task/Event object model, which limits their availability in standard reports, list views, and automation compared to natively created Activities — a real constraint stakeholders must plan around. A is incorrect: EAC explicitly supports Outlook and Gmail connections; that's its core function. C is incorrect: EAC operates within the existing org; no separate org is needed. D is incorrect: EAC is a productivity feature, not a CRM replacement."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "A sales manager wants the system to identify which open opportunities are most likely to close, based on historical patterns. Which feature addresses this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Lead Scoring"), ("B", "Territory-based Forecasting"), ("C", "Einstein Opportunity Scoring"), ("D", "Sales Engagement Analytics")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Einstein Opportunity Scoring analyzes historical won/lost patterns against current opportunity attributes to produce a likelihood-to-close score for open deals — exactly this use case. A is incorrect: Lead Scoring evaluates leads, not open opportunities. B is incorrect: Territory-based Forecasting aggregates pipeline value by territory; it doesn't score individual deal likelihood. D is incorrect: Sales Engagement Analytics measures cadence/outreach performance, not deal-close probability."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "A company wants to surface next-best-action recommendations to reps during active deals. Which AI feature supports this?", options: [("A", "Advanced Currency Management"), ("B", "Duplicate Management"), ("C", "Sales Cloud Einstein Conversation Insights surfacing call-based signals that also drive next-step guidance"), ("D", "Einstein Recommendations / Sales AI next-best-action guidance surfaced on the Opportunity record")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Sales Cloud's AI recommendation capability is designed to analyze deal stage, activity, and engagement to suggest the rep's next action directly within the opportunity workflow. A is incorrect: Currency management is a data/config feature unrelated to action guidance. B is incorrect: Duplicate Management prevents redundant records; it has no recommendation engine."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "A company wants an AI feature to generate a call summary and recommended follow-ups automatically after each sales call. Which feature supports this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Conversation Insights / Call Summarization within Sales Cloud"), ("B", "Web-to-Lead"), ("C", "Territory Management"), ("D", "Opportunity Splits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Call summarization capabilities process recorded or transcribed sales calls to generate a concise summary and suggested next steps automatically, directly matching the requirement. B is incorrect: Web-to-Lead only captures inbound form data; it has no relationship to call recordings. C is incorrect: Territory Management is a data-assignment feature, unrelated to call analysis. D is incorrect: Opportunity Splits allocate revenue credit; they don't generate summaries."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "How are summary formula fields in reports typically used to surface calculated Sales Cloud KPIs?", options: [("A", "They permanently alter the underlying Opportunity Amount field"), ("B", "They compute cross-row calculations (e.g., win rate as won-count ÷ total-count) directly within a report without needing a custom field on the object"), ("C", "They are only available in dashboards, never reports"), ("D", "They require Apex to configure")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Summary formulas operate on aggregated report data (e.g., record counts, sums) to compute derived metrics like win rate on the fly, without touching or requiring new fields on the underlying object. A is incorrect: Summary formulas are report-level calculations; they never modify underlying record data. C is incorrect: Summary formulas are a native reporting feature usable directly in reports, which dashboards then visualize. D is incorrect: They're configured declaratively through the report builder UI, no code required."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A sales director wants a dashboard component showing forecast versus quota by rep for the current quarter. What report type should the consultant configure?", options: [("A", "A Campaign report grouped by status"), ("B", "A Lead report grouped by source"), ("C", "A Forecasting-related report (or Opportunity report with Quota data joined) grouped by owner, summarizing forecasted amount against quota"), ("D", "A Task report grouped by activity type")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Comparing forecast to quota per rep requires a report source that includes both forecasted pipeline value and quota figures, grouped by owner — which forecasting/opportunity-based reporting provides. A is incorrect: Campaign reports track marketing program performance, not rep quota attainment. B is incorrect: Lead reports show lead volume/source, not forecast-to-quota comparison. D is incorrect: Task reports show activity counts, not revenue attainment against quota."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "A sales ops team wants a dashboard visible to all reps that shows each viewer only their own data. Which dashboard feature enables this?", options: [("A", "A separate dashboard cloned for every rep"), ("B", "Dashboard filters set to a hardcoded user"), ("C", "Report folders with restricted sharing"), ("D", "Dynamic Dashboards (\"View dashboard as logged-in user\")")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Dynamic Dashboards run each viewer's dashboard using their own record access, so one shared dashboard automatically shows only the data that viewer can see — no per-rep duplication needed. A is incorrect: Cloning a dashboard per rep doesn't scale and creates ongoing maintenance overhead. B is incorrect: A hardcoded filter value can't dynamically adjust per viewer. C is incorrect: Folder sharing controls who can access the dashboard, not what data each viewer sees within it."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "A company wants sales managers to receive a weekly email of open opportunities nearing their close date, without custom development. Which feature fulfills this?", options: [("A", "Scheduled report subscriptions (\"Subscribe\" on a filtered Opportunity report)"), ("B", "A custom Apex scheduled batch job"), ("C", "Einstein Activity Capture"), ("D", "A scheduled Flow that emails a filtered list of nearing-close-date Opportunities on a recurring interval")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Report subscriptions let a user schedule a filtered report (e.g., close date within X days) to be emailed on a recurring cadence entirely declaratively — no code required. B is incorrect: A custom Apex job accomplishes the same outcome but violates the \"without custom development\" constraint. C is incorrect: Activity Capture logs emails/events; it doesn't send scheduled digest reports."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "When should a consultant recommend CRM Analytics (Tableau CRM) dashboards over native Salesforce reports and dashboards?", options: [("A", "Whenever a company has fewer than 10 users"), ("B", "When the requirement involves large-scale, cross-object data blending, advanced visualizations, or what-if analysis beyond native reporting's aggregation limits"), ("C", "Whenever only Lead data is involved"), ("D", "Never — CRM Analytics is being retired")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "CRM Analytics is designed for advanced use cases — large data volumes, multi-source blending, and interactive exploration — that exceed what native report/dashboard aggregation and joins can deliver. A is incorrect: Small user counts don't determine analytical complexity needs; native reporting may already suffice regardless of headcount. C is incorrect: Single-object Lead reporting is well within native reporting's capability; no need for CRM Analytics. D is incorrect: CRM Analytics remains an active, supported Salesforce product for advanced analytics."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A company is beginning a Sales Cloud implementation with historically resistant sales reps. What change management activity should the consultant recommend before go-live?", options: [("A", "Delay all training until after go-live to avoid overwhelming reps"), ("B", "Restrict system access to managers only during the first month"), ("C", "Identify and train a super-user/champion network in each region to model adoption and answer peer questions"), ("D", "Skip a communication plan since the system change speaks for itself")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A champion network creates embedded peer advocates who model correct usage and field day-to-day questions, which is the most effective lever against pre-existing resistance. A is incorrect: Delaying training until after go-live guarantees frustration and workaround habits form before reps ever see proper usage. B is incorrect: Restricting access to managers only prevents reps from building familiarity and delays adoption further. D is incorrect: Skipping communication ignores that resistant users need a clear \"why\" before they'll change behavior."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "A company has historically low CRM adoption going into a new Sales Cloud rollout. What pre-implementation UX assessment should the consultant perform to address this risk?", options: [("A", "Copy the previous CRM's page layout exactly to minimize change"), ("B", "Assume adoption will improve automatically with a new tool"), ("C", "Only survey the executive sponsor about desired features"), ("D", "Shadow reps in their current workflow to identify friction points and design the new UI/process around their actual daily tasks")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Observing actual rep workflows surfaces the real friction driving low adoption, letting the consultant design a UX that fits how reps actually work rather than how leadership assumes they work. A is incorrect: Replicating the old CRM's layout preserves whatever caused the original low adoption. B is incorrect: New tools without addressing root UX causes rarely fix adoption on their own. C is incorrect: Executive-only input misses the frontline usability issues driving non-adoption."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "A stakeholder group includes executive sponsors, frontline managers, and end users. How should the communication plan differ across these groups during implementation?", options: [("A", "Executives get strategic ROI/timeline updates, managers get operational readiness and team-impact details, and end users get task-level \"what changes for me\" guidance"), ("B", "All groups should receive the identical weekly status email"), ("C", "Only end users need any communication, since they use the system daily"), ("D", "Communication should stop once the design phase ends")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Each stakeholder group makes different decisions and has different concerns, so tailoring content depth and framing to their role keeps engagement relevant and actionable. B is incorrect: A single generic email risks being too detailed for executives and too abstract for end users, losing both audiences. C is incorrect: Executives and managers need visibility to remove blockers and reinforce adoption; skipping them undermines support. D is incorrect: Communication needs to continue through build, test, and go-live to maintain alignment as the solution takes shape."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "A consultant is designing training for a 200-rep, three-region Sales Cloud rollout. What factor should most drive the training strategy?", options: [("A", "One single generic training video sent to all 200 reps regardless of role"), ("B", "Role-based training paths (e.g., rep vs. manager) delivered in region-appropriate timing and format, informed by each region's process variations"), ("C", "Region-specific champions/train-the-trainer sessions to reinforce role-based content in each region's own context"), ("D", "Requiring all reps to complete Trailhead badges as the sole training method")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "At this scale, role differences (what a rep needs to know vs. a manager) and regional process variations both materially affect what \"useful training\" looks like, so the strategy must segment along those lines. A is incorrect: A single generic video ignores role-specific needs and won't stick for a 200-person, three-region rollout. D is incorrect: Requiring Trailhead badges as the sole method ignores role- and region-specific process differences."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "When should a consultant establish a super-user/champion network in a Sales Cloud implementation?", options: [("A", "Only after adoption problems appear post-go-live"), ("B", "Never — champions create shadow IT risk"), ("C", "During the design/build phase, so champions are trained ahead of go-live and can support peers from day one"), ("D", "During the discovery phase, before requirements are even defined")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Establishing champions during build ensures they're proficient and ready to support colleagues immediately at go-live, front-loading adoption support rather than reacting to problems later. A is incorrect: Waiting until adoption problems appear means champions arrive too late to prevent the habits that caused the problem. B is incorrect: Champion networks are a recognized best practice for adoption, not a governance risk. D is incorrect: Champions need a defined solution to learn and champion; establishing the role before requirements exist gives them nothing to learn yet."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "What metrics should a consultant recommend to measure success in the first 90 days after a Sales Cloud go-live?", options: [("A", "Login/adoption rate by user"), ("B", "Percentage of required opportunity fields completed"), ("C", "Total company headcount"), ("D", "Number of AppExchange packages installed"), ("E", "Office square footage utilization")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Login/adoption rate and data-completeness rate directly reflect whether reps are actually using the system and using it correctly — the two most direct proxies for early implementation success. C is incorrect: Headcount is an HR metric unrelated to Sales Cloud usage. D is incorrect: Package count measures tooling breadth, not usage or data quality. E is incorrect: Office utilization has no bearing on CRM adoption."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "What is the key difference between a phased rollout and a big-bang deployment?", options: [("A", "A phased rollout requires no user acceptance testing"), ("B", "A big-bang deployment is always cheaper"), ("C", "They are identical approaches with different names"), ("D", "A phased rollout deploys to subsets of users/regions sequentially to manage risk, while a big-bang deployment launches to all users simultaneously")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The defining distinction is risk exposure timing: phased limits blast radius by rolling out incrementally, while big-bang accepts full simultaneous exposure for a single cutover. A is incorrect: Phased rollouts still require UAT for each phase's scope; that requirement doesn't disappear. B is incorrect: Big-bang can actually cost more if issues surface at full scale simultaneously, requiring firefighting across the whole user base. C is incorrect: The two approaches have materially different risk and coordination profiles."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A company is migrating from sandbox to production for go-live. What deployment consideration should the consultant prioritize to minimize risk?", options: [("A", "Use a validated deployment pipeline (e.g., change sets or DevOps tooling) with full regression testing in a staging/full sandbox before promoting to production"), ("B", "Manually recreate every configuration change directly in production"), ("C", "Skip testing since sandbox and production are identical"), ("D", "Deploy only Apex code and handle declarative metadata separately with no tracking")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A validated pipeline with regression testing in a production-like sandbox catches conflicts and regressions before they reach live users, which is the core risk-minimization practice for go-live promotion. B is incorrect: Manually recreating changes in production introduces human error and bypasses testing entirely. C is incorrect: Sandboxes can diverge from production in data, license, and config state; assuming parity is a common cause of failed deployments. D is incorrect: Splitting code and metadata deployment without tracking risks missed dependencies and inconsistent states."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "A consultant must deploy custom fields, page layouts, validation rules, and workflow rules to production together. Which deployment approach is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Deploying each component type on a different day with no sequencing plan"), ("B", "A single coordinated change set (or DevOps pipeline deployment) containing all interdependent metadata components"), ("C", "Manually re-entering each component directly in production"), ("D", "Using Data Loader to migrate metadata")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "These components are interdependent (e.g., validation rules reference fields, layouts reference fields), so deploying them together in one coordinated package avoids broken references mid-deployment. A is incorrect: Splitting dependent components across days risks temporary broken states (e.g., validation rule referencing a field not yet deployed). C is incorrect: Manual re-entry in production is error-prone and defeats the purpose of a repeatable, testable deployment process. D is incorrect: Data Loader migrates records, not metadata; it cannot deploy fields, layouts, or rules."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "What testing activities should a consultant conduct during a Sales Cloud implementation before promoting to production?", options: [("A", "User Acceptance Testing (UAT) with representative end users"), ("B", "Regression testing of existing automations and integrations"), ("C", "Renaming the production org"), ("D", "Disabling all validation rules permanently"), ("E", "Deleting historical data to speed up testing")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "UAT confirms the solution meets real business needs from the user's perspective, and regression testing confirms new changes haven't broken existing functionality — together these are the core pre-promotion testing activities. C is incorrect: Renaming the org is an administrative action unrelated to testing. D is incorrect: Permanently disabling validation rules removes the very guardrails testing is meant to validate. E is incorrect: Deleting historical data destroys the data testing needs to validate against and risks unrecoverable loss."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A company wants to pilot Sales Cloud in one region before expanding company-wide. What consideration should guide this approach?", options: [("A", "Select the smallest, least representative region purely for speed"), ("B", "Skip gathering pilot feedback since the design is already final"), ("C", "Select a pilot region representative of broader business processes so lessons learned generalize to the full rollout"), ("D", "Roll out to all regions simultaneously despite calling it a \"pilot\"")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A pilot's value depends on its findings transferring to the rest of the rollout, which requires the pilot group's processes to be reasonably representative of the broader organization. A is incorrect: An unrepresentative region may surface issues (or miss issues) that don't reflect what larger regions will experience. B is incorrect: The entire purpose of a pilot is to capture feedback and refine before scaling; skipping this defeats the pilot's value. D is incorrect: Rolling out to everyone simultaneously isn't a pilot at all — it's a big-bang deployment mislabeled."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "What governance structure should a consultant recommend to manage ongoing configuration changes and user access post-implementation?", options: [("A", "Allow any system administrator to make production changes at any time with no review"), ("B", "Freeze all configuration changes permanently after go-live"), ("C", "Route every change request directly to the original implementation partner indefinitely"), ("D", "A Center of Excellence (CoE) or change advisory board with a defined change-request and release process")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A CoE/governance board with a structured intake and release process balances agility with control, ensuring changes are reviewed, tested, and coordinated rather than made ad hoc. A is incorrect: Unrestricted admin changes with no review create risk of conflicting or untested changes reaching production. B is incorrect: A permanent freeze prevents the org from evolving with legitimate, changing business needs. C is incorrect: Routing everything to the original partner indefinitely is unsustainable and doesn't build internal ownership."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "What metrics and review cadence should a consultant establish to support continuous improvement after go-live?", options: [("A", "Regular (e.g., monthly/quarterly) business reviews tracking adoption, data quality, and process KPIs against baseline"), ("B", "A one-time review immediately at go-live with no follow-up"), ("C", "Reviewing only technical error logs, never business metrics"), ("D", "Annual reviews only, regardless of issue severity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Recurring reviews at a reasonable cadence let stakeholders track adoption and data-quality trends over time and catch drift early, which is the essence of continuous improvement. B is incorrect: A single post-go-live review can't detect trends or degradation that emerge over subsequent months. C is incorrect: Technical logs alone miss business-outcome signals like adoption and data quality. D is incorrect: Annual-only cadence is too infrequent to catch and correct issues before they compound."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "Six months after go-live, login rates are low and opportunity records are incomplete. What should the consultant recommend first?", options: [("A", "Immediately mandate stricter validation rules company-wide without investigation"), ("B", "Conduct an adoption diagnostic (surveys, usage reports, rep interviews) to identify root causes before prescribing a fix"), ("C", "Assume the tool is flawed and begin a full re-implementation"), ("D", "Pair the diagnostic with targeted follow-up training or workflow fixes once specific root causes are confirmed")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Diagnosing root causes (training gaps, UX friction, process misfit) before acting ensures the eventual fix addresses the actual problem rather than guessing. A is incorrect: Adding stricter validation without knowing the cause risks worsening frustration if the root issue is, say, poor training rather than lax data entry. C is incorrect: A full re-implementation is a drastic, costly step to take before any root-cause investigation."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "A company's sales process changes significantly after go-live, requiring new stages, fields, and approval workflows. How should the consultant manage this?", options: [("A", "Make the changes directly in production without testing to save time"), ("B", "Refuse to make any changes since go-live has already occurred"), ("C", "Treat it as a new change request through the governance process: gather requirements, test in sandbox, and deploy through the standard release cycle"), ("D", "Re-run the entire original discovery process from scratch")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Post-go-live changes of this magnitude still warrant proper requirements-gathering and sandbox testing before deployment, following the same rigor as the original build — just scoped through ongoing governance rather than a brand-new project. A is incorrect: Skipping testing on approval workflows and stage changes risks breaking active pipeline data and validation logic. B is incorrect: Sales Cloud implementations are expected to evolve with business needs; refusing changes isn't a viable consulting stance. D is incorrect: A full discovery restart is excessive for an incremental process change; the governance/change-request path is proportionate."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "Some reps report seeing opportunities they shouldn't have access to after go-live. What is the recommended first diagnostic step?", options: [("A", "Immediately delete the affected opportunity records"), ("B", "Disable the Opportunity object entirely until further notice"), ("C", "Assume it is a bug in Salesforce core platform and open a case with no internal investigation"), ("D", "Review Organization-Wide Defaults, role hierarchy, and sharing rules on Opportunity to identify where over-broad access originates")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Unexpected visibility almost always traces to OWD, role hierarchy, or sharing rule configuration; reviewing these systematically identifies the actual access-granting mechanism before any remediation. A is incorrect: Deleting records is destructive and does nothing to fix the underlying access misconfiguration. B is incorrect: Disabling the object halts all sales operations, a wildly disproportionate response to a sharing misconfiguration. C is incorrect: Access issues are overwhelmingly caused by org configuration, not platform bugs; skipping internal review wastes time."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "What are the key deliverables typically expected at the design phase of a Salesforce implementation project?", options: [("A", "A solution design document detailing data model, process flows, and configuration approach, validated against business requirements"), ("B", "The final signed contract with the customer"), ("C", "The go-live hypercare support schedule only"), ("D", "A list of Trailhead badges reps must complete")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The design phase's core output is the documented solution approach (data model, process, configuration) that both the delivery team and client validate before building begins. B is incorrect: Contract signing typically precedes the project, not a design-phase deliverable. C is incorrect: Hypercare scheduling belongs to deployment/support planning, much later in the lifecycle. D is incorrect: Trailhead badge lists are a training artifact, not a design deliverable."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "What is the purpose of a RACI matrix in a Salesforce implementation project?", options: [("A", "To track project budget line items exclusively"), ("B", "To clarify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, and Informed for each project activity, reducing ambiguity in ownership"), ("C", "To document Salesforce field-level security settings"), ("D", "To replace the project timeline entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A RACI matrix exists specifically to assign clear roles across project activities, preventing the ambiguity that causes missed deliverables or duplicated effort. A is incorrect: Budget tracking is handled by financial project management tools, not a RACI matrix. C is incorrect: Field-level security is a Salesforce configuration concern, unrelated to project role clarity. D is incorrect: A RACI matrix complements a timeline (who does what) rather than replacing it (when things happen)."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "A Sales Cloud project is at risk of scope creep as stakeholders add requirements mid-build. What technique should the consultant apply?", options: [("A", "Maintain a living scope/requirements document that the change-control log updates whenever a request is approved"), ("B", "Refuse to discuss any new requirements for the rest of the project"), ("C", "Log new requests in a formal change-control process, assessing impact on timeline/budget before approving"), ("D", "Restart the entire project from discovery")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Formal change control lets the team evaluate each new request's impact and make an informed accept/defer/reject decision, protecting the timeline while still allowing legitimate changes. B is incorrect: Blanket refusal is unrealistic and damages the client relationship; some changes may be legitimately necessary. D is incorrect: Restarting from discovery over routine scope changes is a disproportionate, costly overreaction."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "A Sales Cloud project has a fixed deadline but requirements are still being finalized by the client. How should the consultant handle this tension?", options: [("A", "Extend the deadline unilaterally without client sign-off"), ("B", "Attempt to build every possible requirement simultaneously regardless of the deadline"), ("C", "Cancel the project"), ("D", "Define an MVP scope locked early, and defer remaining items to a documented post-launch phase")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Locking a minimum viable scope lets the team hit the fixed deadline with a working solution, while a documented follow-on phase honors remaining requirements without jeopardizing the date. A is incorrect: Unilaterally changing the deadline without client agreement damages trust and project governance. B is incorrect: Trying to build everything simultaneously against a fixed deadline with unfinalized requirements is the direct cause of scope-driven delivery failure. C is incorrect: Cancellation is an extreme response to a manageable scope/timeline tension."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "What is the appropriate approach for a consultant to escalate a risk that could derail the go-live timeline?", options: [("A", "Document the risk with impact/likelihood, then raise it promptly through the project's defined escalation path (e.g., steering committee) with mitigation options"), ("B", "Wait until go-live day to mention it"), ("C", "Resolve it unilaterally without informing the client"), ("D", "Post about it informally in a side Slack channel only")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Timely, documented escalation with proposed mitigation gives stakeholders the lead time to make decisions, which is the core purpose of a risk escalation process. B is incorrect: Waiting until go-live day eliminates any opportunity to mitigate the risk in time. C is incorrect: Resolving unilaterally without informing the client removes their ability to weigh in on a decision affecting their business. D is incorrect: An informal side-channel post lacks the visibility and accountability a formal escalation path provides."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "What is the difference between a functional requirement and a technical requirement in a Sales Cloud implementation?", options: [("A", "They are interchangeable terms for the same document"), ("B", "A functional requirement describes what the business needs the system to do; a technical requirement describes how it will be built to satisfy that need"), ("C", "Functional requirements are only written by developers"), ("D", "Technical requirements are optional and rarely documented")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Functional requirements capture business intent (the \"what\"), while technical requirements translate that intent into implementation specifics (the \"how\") — a foundational distinction for solution design documentation. A is incorrect: Conflating the two loses the traceability between business need and technical solution. C is incorrect: Functional requirements are typically authored or validated by business stakeholders and analysts, not developers. D is incorrect: Technical requirements are essential for build-phase clarity and are standard project deliverables."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "How should a consultant use a process map or swim-lane diagram during discovery?", options: [("A", "Only as a decorative artifact for the final presentation"), ("B", "To replace the need for stakeholder interviews"), ("C", "To visually clarify handoffs and responsibilities between marketing, inside sales, and field sales, exposing gaps or redundancies in the current process"), ("D", "Exclusively to document Salesforce field-level security")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Swim-lane diagrams make cross-team handoffs and ownership explicit, which is exactly what surfaces process gaps and redundancies that stakeholders often can't articulate verbally. A is incorrect: Treating it as decorative wastes its actual analytical value during discovery. B is incorrect: A process map complements interviews by visualizing what's described; it doesn't replace the need to talk to stakeholders. D is incorrect: Field-level security is a technical configuration detail, unrelated to cross-team process mapping."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "A consultant leads a discovery workshop with a sales team that has conflicting regional priorities. How should the consultant facilitate to reach alignment?", options: [("A", "Let the loudest stakeholder's preferences dictate the final requirements"), ("B", "Avoid discussing the conflict and document all requests as equally critical"), ("C", "Document the agreed prioritization and trade-off rationale so regions can see how their input shaped the outcome"), ("D", "Surface each region's underlying business drivers, then facilitate a structured prioritization exercise (e.g., MoSCoW) tied to overall company goals")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Understanding underlying drivers and applying a structured prioritization framework anchored to shared company goals gives the group an objective basis for trade-offs, rather than politics deciding the outcome. A is incorrect: Letting the loudest voice win ignores actual business drivers and breeds resentment among quieter regions. B is incorrect: Avoiding the conflict just defers it and produces an unusable requirements list with no real priorities."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "What techniques should a consultant use during discovery to surface implicit requirements stakeholders haven't explicitly stated?", options: [("A", "Ask \"why\" and \"what happens if\" probing questions, observe actual workflows, and review exception/edge-case scenarios stakeholders take for granted"), ("B", "Only accept the requirements written in the initial statement of work"), ("C", "Assume no additional requirements exist beyond what's stated in the kickoff email"), ("D", "Skip discovery entirely and begin building based on the sales pitch deck")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Implicit requirements often live in habits and exceptions stakeholders consider \"obvious\" and don't think to mention; probing questions and workflow observation are the standard techniques to surface them. B is incorrect: A statement of work is typically high-level and rarely captures every implicit process detail. C is incorrect: Assuming no gaps exist beyond an initial email virtually guarantees missed requirements. D is incorrect: Skipping discovery and building from a sales deck risks a solution disconnected from actual operational needs."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "During discovery, the consultant finds the client's current CRM process is undocumented and inconsistent across teams. What should the consultant do before designing the solution?", options: [("A", "Design the solution based on whichever team's process the consultant encountered first"), ("B", "Facilitate cross-team workshops to document and reconcile a target-state process before mapping it into Salesforce configuration"), ("C", "Proceed directly to build, assuming Salesforce defaults will resolve the inconsistency"), ("D", "Postpone the project indefinitely until the client documents its own processes independently")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Reconciling a target-state process across teams before configuration ensures the solution reflects an agreed, coherent process rather than encoding one team's undocumented habits as \"the\" process. A is incorrect: Picking one team's process arbitrarily risks building a solution that fights the other teams' actual workflows. C is incorrect: Salesforce defaults don't resolve business process inconsistency; that's an organizational design problem, not a configuration one. D is incorrect: Facilitating this reconciliation is core consulting work; postponing indefinitely abdicates that responsibility."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "A company presents 30 feature requests for a Sales Cloud implementation. How should the consultant prioritize these to define MVP scope?", options: [("A", "Build all 30 requests simultaneously in the first release"), ("B", "Implement requests in the order they were submitted"), ("C", "Score each request against business impact and implementation effort/complexity, then group into MVP vs. later phases"), ("D", "Let the developer with the most availability choose which requests to build")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "An impact-versus-effort prioritization framework objectively separates must-haves from nice-to-haves, which is the standard way to define a defensible MVP scope from a long request list. A is incorrect: Building all 30 simultaneously abandons the concept of a scoped MVP and risks timeline failure. B is incorrect: Submission order has no correlation to business value or complexity. D is incorrect: Developer availability is a resourcing factor, not a valid basis for business prioritization."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "What happens to standard lead field values when a lead is converted in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "All lead field values are permanently deleted upon conversion"), ("B", "Lead fields convert only to the Opportunity, never Account or Contact"), ("C", "Conversion requires manually re-entering every field on the new records"), ("D", "Mapped standard fields copy onto the newly created Account, Contact, and Opportunity records according to lead conversion field mapping")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Lead conversion mapping copies data from the Lead into the corresponding Account, Contact, and Opportunity fields automatically, preserving captured information without re-entry. A is incorrect: Field values are copied forward, not deleted; the original Lead record is retained (marked converted) with its data intact. B is incorrect: Conversion creates and populates Account and Contact records too, not just Opportunity. C is incorrect: The mapping automates field population; manual re-entry defeats the purpose of conversion mapping."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "How can a consultant map custom lead fields to contact, account, and opportunity fields during conversion?", options: [("A", "Configure Lead Field Mapping in Setup to associate each custom Lead field with its corresponding custom field on the target object"), ("B", "Custom fields cannot be mapped during lead conversion under any circumstance"), ("C", "Validate the mapped values post-conversion with a report comparing source Lead data to the resulting Account/Contact/Opportunity fields"), ("D", "Field mapping only works for Salesforce standard fields")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Setup's Lead Field Mapping tool explicitly supports associating custom Lead fields with matching custom fields on Account, Contact, and Opportunity for automatic population at conversion. B is incorrect: Custom field mapping is a supported, common configuration step for lead conversion. D is incorrect: Field mapping supports both standard and custom fields during conversion."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "A company uses Web-to-Lead to capture inbound web inquiries. What should the consultant flag regarding configuration and limits?", options: [("A", "Web-to-Lead has no submission limits whatsoever"), ("B", "Web-to-Lead has a default daily submission limit per org, and the generated HTML form must be hosted and secured (e.g., against spam) outside default validation"), ("C", "Web-to-Lead automatically creates Opportunities, not Leads"), ("D", "Web-to-Lead requires a paid third-party app to function")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Web-to-Lead is subject to a daily lead-generation limit set by Salesforce and, since it exposes an open web form, needs additional measures (e.g., reCAPTCHA, validation rules) to guard against spam submissions — both are real considerations a consultant must flag. A is incorrect: An unlimited assumption risks silently dropped submissions once the daily cap is hit. C is incorrect: Web-to-Lead creates Lead records, not Opportunities, by design. D is incorrect: Web-to-Lead is a native, no-cost Salesforce feature; no third-party app is required."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "A company wants Web-to-Lead submissions auto-routed by lead source, geography, and product interest. Which features apply, and in what order are they evaluated?", options: [("A", "Sharing Rules, evaluated randomly"), ("B", "Duplicate Rules, evaluated bottom-up"), ("C", "Lead Assignment Rules, evaluated top-down by rule entry order, with the first matching rule entry determining assignment"), ("D", "Validation Rules, evaluated alphabetically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Lead Assignment Rules evaluate their ordered rule entries from top to bottom and apply the first one whose criteria match, which is exactly the mechanism used to route by source, geography, and product interest. A is incorrect: Sharing Rules grant record visibility; they don't perform initial assignment/routing logic. B is incorrect: Duplicate Rules detect matching records; they play no role in routing new leads. D is incorrect: Validation Rules block invalid data entry; they have no routing function and no alphabetical evaluation order."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "What is the difference between opportunity stages and sales processes in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "They are the same configuration object with different labels"), ("B", "Sales processes define page layouts, not stage progression"), ("C", "Stages can exist without being part of any sales process"), ("D", "A sales process is an ordered set of stages assigned to a record type, while stages are the individual steps within that process")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A Sales Process is the picklist-value-ordered container of Stages, and it's this Sales Process that gets tied to a Record Type — stages are the building blocks, the process is the assembled sequence. A is incorrect: They're distinct configuration concepts: Stage values vs. the ordered Sales Process object that arranges them. B is incorrect: Page layouts are assigned separately via Record Type, not directly by Sales Process. C is incorrect: Every Stage value used on an Opportunity must belong to at least the org's picklist, and is meaningfully organized only when part of a Sales Process."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "How are opportunity stages and sales processes related to record types?", options: [("A", "A Record Type is assigned a specific Sales Process, which determines which Stage values are available and their default order for opportunities of that record type"), ("B", "Record types have no relationship to sales processes"), ("C", "Every record type must share the exact same sales process"), ("D", "Sales processes are assigned to Profiles, not Record Types")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Salesforce's model links Record Type → Sales Process → Stage picklist values, letting different opportunity types (e.g., New Business vs. Renewal) follow different stage progressions. B is incorrect: This is precisely the relationship that lets different business processes coexist on one object. C is incorrect: Forcing one shared process across all record types defeats the purpose of having multiple record types. D is incorrect: Sales Processes are assigned at the Record Type level (via Support Business Process assignment), not directly to Profiles."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "A company wants every converted lead to create an opportunity with a specific record type and auto-populated required fields. How should the consultant configure this?", options: [("A", "Manually instruct each rep to select the record type after conversion"), ("B", "Set the lead conversion default Opportunity record type (or use a Flow to set it dynamically) and configure field mapping/defaults for required fields"), ("C", "Disable Opportunity creation during lead conversion entirely"), ("D", "Add validation rules on the required fields so incomplete conversions surface immediately if defaults are missing")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Combining a defined (or Flow-driven dynamic) record type default with field mapping ensures every converted lead consistently produces a correctly typed, populated Opportunity without manual steps. A is incorrect: Manual selection introduces inconsistency and defeats the automation goal. C is incorrect: Disabling Opportunity creation contradicts the stated requirement to create one on every conversion."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "A company needs to track multiple contacts on one opportunity in different roles (economic buyer, technical buyer, champion). Which feature supports this?", options: [("A", "Opportunity Teams"), ("B", "Account Teams"), ("C", "Contact Roles on the Opportunity"), ("D", "Campaign Members")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Contact Roles is the native junction object purpose-built to associate multiple contacts with an opportunity, each tagged with a role label like Economic Buyer or Champion. A is incorrect: Opportunity Teams track internal Salesforce users collaborating on the deal, not external buying-committee contacts. B is incorrect: Account Teams manage internal collaborators at the account level, not contact-role tracking on deals. D is incorrect: Campaign Members track marketing campaign participation, not buying-committee roles on an opportunity."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "A sales team wants opportunity stages to enforce exit criteria before reps can advance to the next stage. Which features support this?", options: [("A", "Opportunity Splits"), ("B", "Campaign Influence"), ("C", "Advanced Currency Management"), ("D", "Validation rules (or Flow) tied to required fields/checkboxes representing stage exit criteria")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Validation rules (or a Flow triggered on stage change) can require specific fields or checkboxes to be completed before allowing a stage transition, effectively enforcing exit criteria declaratively. A is incorrect: Opportunity Splits allocate revenue credit among team members; they don't gate stage progression. B is incorrect: Campaign Influence tracks marketing attribution, unrelated to stage-gating logic. C is incorrect: Currency management handles exchange rates, with no bearing on stage validation."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "A company wants reps to follow a guided selling path with different questions at each opportunity stage. Which feature best supports this?", options: [("A", "Path (with Guidance for Success) configured per stage on the Opportunity"), ("B", "Territory Model"), ("C", "Duplicate Rules"), ("D", "Advanced Currency Management")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Path lets a consultant define stage-specific guidance and key fields, giving reps a lightweight guided-selling experience tailored to each stage's questions and priorities. B is incorrect: Territory Model governs account/opportunity assignment structure, not in-record UI guidance. C is incorrect: Duplicate Rules manage record matching, unrelated to guided selling. D is incorrect: Currency management is a data configuration feature with no UI guidance role."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A company syncs quotes to opportunities. What is the impact on the opportunity's amount and line items when the quote is synced?", options: [("A", "Syncing a quote has no effect on the Opportunity record at all"), ("B", "The synced quote's line items become the opportunity's line items, and the Opportunity Amount updates to reflect the quote's total"), ("C", "Syncing a quote deletes the Opportunity record"), ("D", "The Opportunity Amount is locked permanently once any quote is created")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When a Quote is marked as the synced quote, its Quote Line Items push down to become the Opportunity's Line Items, and the rolled-up total updates the Opportunity Amount — keeping them in lockstep. A is incorrect: Syncing is specifically designed to propagate quote data onto the opportunity; claiming no effect misstates the core feature behavior. C is incorrect: Syncing updates data; it never deletes the Opportunity record. D is incorrect: The Amount remains editable/dynamic and continues updating as the synced quote's line items change."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "A company requires deals above $50,000 to go through a discount-approval process before the quote is sent. How should the consultant design this?", options: [("A", "A validation rule preventing the quote from being marked \"Presented\" until the linked approval is fully approved"), ("B", "Manual email approval outside of Salesforce"), ("C", "An Approval Process on the Quote (or Opportunity) triggered by a discount/amount threshold, routing to the appropriate approver before the quote can be marked as presented"), ("D", "A sharing rule restricting quote visibility")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Salesforce's native Approval Process feature is built exactly for conditional, threshold-based sign-off workflows like discount approval, with defined approvers and status tracking on the record itself. B is incorrect: Manual email approval outside Salesforce loses status tracking and auditability on the record itself. D is incorrect: A sharing rule only controls visibility; it has no approval-routing or blocking capability."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "What is the difference between Opportunity Teams and Account Teams?", options: [("A", "They are functionally identical with no configuration difference"), ("B", "Account Teams can only include external partner users"), ("C", "Opportunity Teams apply automatically to every opportunity company-wide with no setup"), ("D", "Opportunity Teams are collaborators assigned per individual deal, while Account Teams are collaborators assigned at the account level and can optionally auto-populate to new opportunities")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Account Teams persist at the account level and can be configured to auto-add to opportunities under that account, while Opportunity Teams are set per-deal — the scope of assignment is the key distinction. A is incorrect: Their scope (account-level vs. opportunity-level) and default propagation behavior differ meaningfully. B is incorrect: Account Teams can include any internal user with appropriate access; they aren't restricted to external partners. C is incorrect: Opportunity Teams require explicit assignment (manually or via Account Team default settings); they don't populate automatically without configuration."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "When should a consultant recommend Opportunity Teams versus Account Teams?", options: [("A", "Account Teams when the same group consistently supports all deals for an account; Opportunity Teams when collaborator needs vary deal by deal"), ("B", "Always recommend Opportunity Teams, never Account Teams"), ("C", "Always recommend Account Teams, never Opportunity Teams"), ("D", "The choice depends solely on the user's license type")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The right choice hinges on whether collaboration needs are consistent at the account level (favoring Account Teams' default propagation) or vary per deal (favoring ad hoc Opportunity Teams). B is incorrect: Always defaulting to Opportunity Teams ignores cases where the same team supports every deal on an account, creating repetitive manual work. C is incorrect: Always defaulting to Account Teams ignores deals that genuinely need a unique collaborator set. D is incorrect: License type determines feature access broadly, but doesn't determine which team model fits the actual collaboration pattern."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "A company's OWD for Opportunities is Private, but sales managers need to see all opportunities owned by their direct reports. What is the correct configuration?", options: [("A", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write"), ("B", "Rely on the standard role hierarchy, which automatically grants managers visibility into records owned by users below them, as long as it's not overridden"), ("C", "Manually share every individual opportunity record"), ("D", "Disable OWD entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Role hierarchy access is additive to OWD by default: a manager's role automatically inherits visibility into records owned by roles beneath it, satisfying this exact requirement without loosening org-wide access. A is incorrect: Opening OWD to Public Read/Write grants access to everyone, not just direct managers, violating the Private intent. C is incorrect: Manual per-record sharing doesn't scale and isn't needed since role hierarchy already provides this access. D is incorrect: OWD can't be \"disabled\"; it's the baseline access level and removing restriction defeats its purpose."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "Regional sales managers need to see all opportunities in their region only; reps see only their own. Which security configuration achieves this?", options: [("A", "A single flat role for all users"), ("B", "Public Read/Write OWD with no hierarchy"), ("C", "A role hierarchy structured by region (or Enterprise Territory Management) with OWD set to Private"), ("D", "Field-level security on the Amount field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A region-aligned role hierarchy (or territory model) under Private OWD naturally grants each regional manager visibility into their region's reps' records while keeping other regions restricted — exactly matching the requirement. A is incorrect: A flat role structure gives no differentiated visibility between regions or between reps and managers. B is incorrect: Public Read/Write grants everyone access to everything, defeating the region-scoped restriction. D is incorrect: Field-level security governs field visibility, not record-level access by region."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "A company wants to grant specific feature access (e.g., Export Reports) to a subset of reps without changing their profile. What is the recommended approach?", options: [("A", "Clone the profile for every user needing the permission"), ("B", "Grant System Administrator profile to those users temporarily"), ("C", "Use a Permission Set Group to bundle this permission with other related incremental grants for easier assignment"), ("D", "Assign a Permission Set (or Permission Set Group) granting that specific permission to the affected users")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Permission Sets are designed exactly for this: granting incremental permissions to a subset of users without altering their base profile or requiring a profile proliferation. A is incorrect: Cloning profiles per exception creates unnecessary profile sprawl and maintenance burden. B is incorrect: Granting admin access is a massive over-grant of privilege for a single reporting permission."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "What are permission set groups, and when should a consultant use them instead of individual permission sets?", options: [("A", "Permission Set Groups bundle multiple related permission sets into one assignable unit — use them when a role consistently needs the same combination of permission sets together"), ("B", "Permission Set Groups replace profiles entirely and eliminate the need for any profile"), ("C", "Permission Set Groups can only contain one permission set each"), ("D", "Permission Set Groups are used exclusively for object-level sharing rules")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Grouping frequently co-assigned permission sets into a single Permission Set Group simplifies administration when a user population consistently needs the same bundle of access. B is incorrect: Profiles remain required as the baseline; Permission Set Groups supplement, not replace, profiles. C is incorrect: Permission Set Groups are explicitly designed to bundle multiple permission sets, not just one. D is incorrect: They manage system/object/field permissions, not record-sharing rules."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "A cross-functional team needs temporary access to a set of strategic accounts, without changing OWD or role hierarchy. What is the best approach?", options: [("A", "Change the OWD for Accounts to Public Read/Write"), ("B", "A manual sharing rule or ad hoc record sharing (or a temporary sharing rule based on criteria) scoped to those specific accounts"), ("C", "Move the team into the top of the role hierarchy"), ("D", "Create a new profile that has System Administrator access")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Targeted manual/ad hoc sharing (or a criteria-based sharing rule scoped narrowly) grants access to exactly the specified accounts without loosening the broader org-wide default or restructuring the hierarchy. A is incorrect: Changing OWD org-wide grants access to all accounts for all users, far beyond the specific temporary need. C is incorrect: Restructuring the role hierarchy is a permanent, broad change disproportionate to a temporary access need. D is incorrect: Granting admin profile access wildly over-provisions privilege beyond what strategic-account access requires."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "A company uses a partner channel where partner reps should see only their own accounts/opportunities in an org shared with internal reps. How should the security model be structured?", options: [("A", "Give partner users the same internal Sales Cloud profile as employees"), ("B", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write for all Accounts and Opportunities"), ("C", "Partner Community/Experience Cloud licenses with Private OWD, sharing rules or sharing sets scoped to the partner's own account records"), ("D", "Deny partners any Salesforce access and use email only")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Partner licenses paired with Private OWD and scoped sharing rules/sets ensure partner users see only records relevant to them, keeping internal and partner visibility properly separated. A is incorrect: Giving partners internal profiles risks over-exposing internal-only data and doesn't align with partner licensing models. B is incorrect: Public Read/Write would let every partner see every account and opportunity company-wide, violating the isolation requirement. D is incorrect: Denying Salesforce access contradicts the stated goal of partners viewing their own records within the org."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "How does territory assignment affect opportunity visibility, and what is the relationship between territory membership and role hierarchy?", options: [("A", "Territory assignment completely replaces the role hierarchy and OWD"), ("B", "Territory membership has zero effect on opportunity visibility"), ("C", "Only the top-level role in the hierarchy can be assigned to territories"), ("D", "Territory membership can grant opportunity access independently of role hierarchy, and Enterprise Territory Management access works alongside — not as a replacement for — the role hierarchy")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Enterprise Territory Management grants opportunity visibility through territory membership as a parallel access path, layered on top of — not replacing — the standard role hierarchy and org-wide defaults. A is incorrect: Territory Management supplements, rather than replaces, the existing role hierarchy and OWD model. B is incorrect: Territory membership is specifically designed to grant visibility into associated opportunities. C is incorrect: Any role level can be involved in territory assignment depending on org design; it isn't restricted to the top role."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "When is it appropriate to use Advanced Currency Management?", options: [("A", "When a multi-currency org needs historically accurate reporting reflecting the exchange rate at each deal's close date, rather than only the current rate"), ("B", "When the org operates in a single currency only"), ("C", "When the company wants to disable multi-currency reporting"), ("D", "Only for Lead conversion scenarios")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Advanced Currency Management exists precisely to preserve historically accurate currency conversion for reporting across time, which matters for multi-currency orgs analyzing trends across different periods' exchange rates. B is incorrect: A single-currency org has no exchange-rate conversion need at all. C is incorrect: It enhances multi-currency reporting accuracy; it doesn't disable it. D is incorrect: It applies broadly to currency-enabled objects, not narrowly to lead conversion."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "A company operates in 15 currencies and wants all opportunities rolled up in USD for reporting. What is required, and what is a limitation?", options: [("A", "Enable multi-currency with a defined corporate currency (USD) for rollups; a limitation is that standard rollup reports use a single static conversion rate unless Advanced Currency Management is also enabled"), ("B", "No configuration is needed; Salesforce converts currencies automatically with no setup"), ("C", "Enable Advanced Currency Management if historically accurate, dated-rate rollups (not just current-rate) are also required"), ("D", "Each currency requires a separate Salesforce org")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Multi-currency must be enabled with a designated corporate currency for consistent USD rollups, and without Advanced Currency Management, conversions use the current static rate rather than dated historical rates — a real limitation to flag. B is incorrect: Multi-currency requires explicit setup and a defined corporate currency; it isn't automatic. D is incorrect: All currencies can coexist within a single multi-currency-enabled org; separate orgs aren't required."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "What happens to the opportunity amount when a synced quote is updated with new products or discounts?", options: [("A", "The Opportunity's line items and Amount automatically update to reflect the synced quote's latest line items and totals"), ("B", "The Opportunity Amount remains frozen at its original value"), ("C", "The quote update is discarded automatically"), ("D", "A new Opportunity record is created for each quote change")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Because sync keeps the Opportunity and Quote line items linked, any update to the synced quote's products or discounts flows through and updates the Opportunity's line items and Amount in real time. B is incorrect: Amount is designed to track the synced quote's current total, not stay frozen. C is incorrect: Updates to a synced quote are applied, not discarded, as part of the sync behavior. D is incorrect: Sync updates the existing Opportunity; it doesn't spawn a new record per change."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "A company wants reps to add products to opportunities and generate a PDF quote from within Salesforce. What steps/considerations are needed?", options: [("A", "Enable Products/Price Books on Opportunities, add Opportunity Line Items, create a Quote from the Opportunity, and configure a Quote Template for the PDF output"), ("B", "Use only Data Loader to generate the PDF"), ("C", "This requires a separate CPQ license and cannot be done with core Sales Cloud"), ("D", "Quotes cannot generate PDFs without custom Apex code")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Standard Sales Cloud Quotes functionality natively supports generating a PDF from a configurable Quote Template once products and quotes are enabled — no CPQ or custom code required for this baseline capability. B is incorrect: Data Loader moves records; it has no PDF generation capability. C is incorrect: Standard Quotes (with PDF templates) ships in core Sales Cloud; CPQ is a separate, more advanced product for complex pricing, not required for basic PDF quotes. D is incorrect: Quote Templates natively generate PDFs declaratively; no Apex is required for standard use cases."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "What are the design considerations when implementing Quotes, particularly around syncing to opportunity line items?", options: [("A", "Only one Quote per Opportunity can be the synced quote at a time, and syncing overwrites the Opportunity's existing line items with the quote's line items"), ("B", "All quotes for an opportunity sync simultaneously with no conflicts"), ("C", "Syncing a quote has no effect on existing opportunity line items"), ("D", "Quotes cannot be created until the Opportunity is Closed Won")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Salesforce enforces a single active synced quote per opportunity, and the sync operation replaces existing opportunity line items with the quote's — both are important behaviors to design around, especially with multiple quote versions. B is incorrect: Only one quote can be synced at a time; multiple simultaneous syncs would create data conflicts, so Salesforce prevents this. C is incorrect: Syncing directly overwrites opportunity line items with the quote's, a significant behavior change. D is incorrect: Quotes can be created and synced throughout the sales cycle, well before Closed Won."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "A company has a 10,000-SKU catalog. What design considerations apply to the product/price book structure to maintain usability?", options: [("A", "Use Product Categories/Families, search-friendly naming, and potentially multiple targeted Price Books rather than one flat, giant catalog list"), ("B", "Put all 10,000 SKUs into a single flat Price Book with no categorization"), ("C", "Limit the org to fewer than 100 total products regardless of catalog"), ("D", "Store products only as a static picklist on the Opportunity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Categorization, clear naming, and segmented price books make a large catalog navigable for reps adding products to opportunities, directly addressing the usability risk of scale. B is incorrect: A single flat list of 10,000 items is unusable for reps trying to find the right product quickly. C is incorrect: Arbitrarily capping the catalog ignores the legitimate business need to represent the full 10,000-SKU range. D is incorrect: A static picklist cannot scale to 10,000 values and lacks pricing/catalog structure entirely."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "What is the purpose of the Lead Status field, and how should it differentiate MQLs from SQLs?", options: [("A", "Lead Status tracks a lead's progress through qualification, with distinct values (e.g., \"Marketing Qualified\", \"Sales Qualified\") configured to reflect each stage's ownership and criteria"), ("B", "Lead Status is a free-text field with no defined values"), ("C", "Lead Status only matters after lead conversion"), ("D", "Pair Lead Status with a Lead Score threshold so marketing- and sales-qualified stages have both a status label and a numeric readiness signal")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "A well-configured Lead Status picklist with distinct MQL/SQL-equivalent values gives visibility into where a lead sits in the qualification funnel and who owns moving it forward — directly supporting the marketing-to-sales handoff. B is incorrect: Lead Status is a standard picklist field with configurable values, not free text. C is incorrect: Lead Status is useful throughout the qualification lifecycle, not only after conversion."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "What are the considerations for using lead queues versus assignment rules for distributing inbound leads?", options: [("A", "Queues let multiple reps pull from a shared pool (good for round-robin/first-come models), while assignment rules deterministically route to a specific owner based on criteria"), ("B", "Queues and assignment rules are mutually exclusive and can never be used together"), ("C", "Assignment rules can only assign to queues, never to individual users"), ("D", "Queues require Apex to configure")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Queues suit a pull-based, shared-pool distribution model, while assignment rules deterministically push a lead to a specific user or queue based on defined criteria — the choice depends on whether the business wants pull or push distribution. B is incorrect: Assignment rules frequently route leads into queues as one of their outcomes, so the two work together. C is incorrect: Assignment rules can assign to either an individual user or a queue. D is incorrect: Queues are configured declaratively in Setup; no Apex is required."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "A company requires duplicate leads be identified and merged before conversion. Which features support this?", options: [("A", "Matching Rules and Duplicate Rules, with Salesforce's native Merge Leads capability applied to the identified duplicates"), ("B", "Advanced Currency Management"), ("C", "Path"), ("D", "Opportunity Splits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Matching Rules identify likely duplicate Leads, Duplicate Rules define what happens when a duplicate is detected (alert/block), and the native Merge Leads action then consolidates them — together covering detection through resolution. B is incorrect: Currency management has no relation to duplicate detection. C is incorrect: Path is a UI guidance feature, unrelated to deduplication. D is incorrect: Opportunity Splits allocate revenue credit; irrelevant to lead duplicate management."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "What is the difference between Lead Source on a Lead and campaign influence on an Opportunity, and when does each matter for reporting?", options: [("A", "They are the same field synced automatically between Lead and Opportunity"), ("B", "Lead Source captures a single originating channel at lead creation, while campaign influence tracks multiple campaigns' contribution to an opportunity over time — Lead Source matters for top-of-funnel origin reporting, campaign influence for multi-touch revenue attribution"), ("C", "Campaign influence only applies to Leads, never Opportunities"), ("D", "Lead Source updates automatically every time a new campaign touches the record")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Lead Source is a single static snapshot of origin at creation time, whereas Campaign Influence is designed to capture multiple, ongoing touches contributing to a deal — they answer different reporting questions (origin vs. multi-touch attribution). A is incorrect: They're distinct fields/mechanisms on different objects, not a synced single value. C is incorrect: Campaign Influence is specifically an Opportunity-level relationship to Campaigns. D is incorrect: Lead Source is generally set once at creation and doesn't auto-update with every subsequent campaign touch."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "How should Campaign Member statuses be configured to support meaningful reporting on event attendance, email responses, and web form submissions?", options: [("A", "Use only the two default statuses (\"Sent\", \"Responded\") for every campaign type"), ("B", "Campaign Member status cannot be customized"), ("C", "Define status values specific to each campaign type (e.g., \"Registered\", \"Attended\", \"No Show\" for events) rather than reusing one generic status set"), ("D", "Campaign Member status has no effect on reporting")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Tailoring status values to each campaign type captures the meaningful distinctions (attended vs. registered vs. no-show) that generic defaults can't, enabling accurate funnel and engagement reporting per channel. A is incorrect: Reusing only the two generic defaults across all campaign types loses the nuance needed for accurate engagement reporting. B is incorrect: Campaign Member status values are fully customizable per campaign. D is incorrect: Status is a primary dimension used for grouping and filtering campaign performance reports."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "A company wants to track which campaigns influenced a closed deal, even when a lead was touched by multiple campaigns. Which feature supports multi-touch attribution?", options: [("A", "Lead Source field alone"), ("B", "A single Campaign lookup field on the Opportunity"), ("C", "Opportunity Contact Roles combined with Campaign Members to trace which contacts and which campaigns touched the deal"), ("D", "Customizable Campaign Influence with a multi-touch attribution model (e.g., First Touch, Last Touch, Even Distribution, or Custom Model)")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Customizable Campaign Influence explicitly supports multiple attribution models designed to distribute credit across several campaigns that touched the same opportunity, directly matching the multi-touch requirement. A is incorrect: Lead Source captures only a single origin value, not multi-campaign attribution. B is incorrect: A single lookup field can reference only one campaign, losing the multi-touch picture."),
        Question(id: "91", question: "A company wants Campaign Hierarchies to roll up ROI from child event campaigns to a parent annual series. How should this be configured, and what is a limitation?", options: [("A", "Set each child campaign's Parent Campaign field to the annual series campaign; a limitation is that hierarchy rollups have a maximum nesting depth and rollup summary fields must be explicitly configured"), ("B", "Campaign Hierarchies roll up automatically with unlimited depth and no configuration"), ("C", "Campaign Hierarchies cannot roll up any numeric fields"), ("D", "Only Opportunities can have parent/child hierarchy relationships, not Campaigns")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Parent Campaign assignment establishes the hierarchy, but Salesforce enforces a hierarchy depth limit and requires explicit rollup summary configuration for metrics like ROI to actually aggregate upward — both are real constraints to plan for. B is incorrect: Rollups require deliberate configuration and are subject to a depth limit; they aren't automatic or unlimited. C is incorrect: Rollup summary fields on campaigns can aggregate numeric fields, including budget/ROI-related figures. D is incorrect: Campaigns natively support Parent Campaign hierarchy relationships, separate from Opportunities."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "A company's sales process differs for new business versus renewal opportunities. How should the consultant configure Salesforce to support both?", options: [("A", "Use one shared record type and manually track the difference in the Description field"), ("B", "Create separate Record Types (and Sales Processes) for New Business and Renewal Opportunities, each with tailored stages and page layouts"), ("C", "Model renewals as a completely separate object unrelated to Opportunity"), ("D", "Disable Opportunity Stage tracking for renewals")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Separate Record Types with distinct Sales Processes let renewal-specific stages (e.g., skipping early-funnel stages) coexist with new-business stages on the same object, each with appropriate layouts. A is incorrect: Tracking the distinction in a free-text field loses the structured stage/process differentiation and breaks clean reporting. C is incorrect: A separate object loses the benefit of unified Opportunity pipeline reporting and standard forecasting. D is incorrect: Disabling stage tracking removes the velocity and forecast-category data renewals still need."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "What is the key difference between Data Loader and the Data Import Wizard for a Sales Cloud migration?", options: [("A", "They are functionally identical with no differences"), ("B", "The Import Wizard supports Apex triggers bypass, while Data Loader does not"), ("C", "Data Loader supports bulk operations beyond the Import Wizard's row limits and object scope, including delete/upsert via API, while the Wizard offers a simpler UI limited to a smaller set of standard/custom objects and record counts"), ("D", "Data Loader can only be used for Leads")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Data Loader is a higher-capacity, API-driven tool supporting more objects and operations (including delete, hard delete) at scale, whereas the Import Wizard is a simpler guided UI intended for smaller, standard-object imports. A is incorrect: Their capacity, supported objects, and operation types differ substantially. B is incorrect: Neither tool inherently bypasses triggers by default; that behavior depends on API settings, not which tool is chosen. D is incorrect: Data Loader works across virtually any standard or custom object, not just Leads."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "When should a consultant use Data Loader versus the Data Import Wizard for a migration?", options: [("A", "Always use the Import Wizard regardless of volume"), ("B", "Always use Data Loader even for a 50-record one-time import by a business user"), ("C", "The choice depends only on the org's Salesforce edition"), ("D", "Data Loader for large volumes, custom objects, or scheduled/automated loads; the Import Wizard for smaller, one-off loads of standard objects by non-technical users")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Matching tool to scale and audience is the practical guideline: Data Loader for volume/complexity/automation, the Wizard for simple, guided, small-scale loads. A is incorrect: The Wizard's UI and object/volume constraints make it impractical for large or complex migrations. B is incorrect: Using Data Loader for a trivial 50-record load is unnecessary overhead compared to the simpler Wizard. C is incorrect: Org edition affects available features generally, but doesn't dictate which import tool best fits a specific migration's scale."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "What is the difference between a full data migration and a delta migration, and when is each appropriate?", options: [("A", "A full migration moves the entire dataset at once (appropriate for initial cutover), while a delta migration moves only changed/new records since the last load (appropriate for keeping systems in sync during a phased transition)"), ("B", "A delta migration always moves more data than a full migration"), ("C", "Run a full migration for the initial cutover, then switch to delta loads for ongoing incremental sync during the transition period"), ("D", "There is no practical difference between the two approaches")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Full migrations suit a one-time complete cutover, while delta migrations minimize load and avoid duplicate processing when systems need to stay synchronized incrementally over a transition period. B is incorrect: A delta migration by definition moves only the incremental subset of changed data, less than a full migration, not more. D is incorrect: The two approaches serve materially different purposes and are not interchangeable."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "A company is migrating 5 years of opportunity history from a legacy CRM. What are key considerations the consultant should address?", options: [("A", "Simply exporting a CSV and importing it directly with no validation"), ("B", "Field mapping accuracy, data cleansing/deduplication before load, preserving historical stage/date accuracy, and load sequencing to respect object dependencies (Accounts/Contacts before Opportunities)"), ("C", "Migrating only the most recent year and discarding the rest automatically"), ("D", "Skipping field mapping since Salesforce infers it automatically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A large historical migration needs deliberate attention to data quality, accurate field mapping, and correct load order (parent objects first) to avoid broken relationships or corrupted historical reporting. A is incorrect: Importing without validation risks propagating legacy data-quality issues directly into the new org. C is incorrect: Discarding four of five years without being asked contradicts the stated migration scope. D is incorrect: Salesforce does not auto-infer field mapping for arbitrary legacy CRM exports; mapping must be defined explicitly."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "What are the considerations for preserving record ownership and historical audit trails when migrating data into Salesforce?", options: [("A", "Ownership and audit history cannot be preserved during any Salesforce migration"), ("B", "Simply assign all migrated records to the system administrator permanently"), ("C", "Use the CreatedDate/LastModifiedDate audit fields (enabled via API for authorized migration users) and map legacy owner identifiers to corresponding Salesforce users to preserve historical accuracy"), ("D", "Audit trail preservation requires a separate paid add-on product")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Salesforce allows API-level control of audit fields like CreatedDate for users with the appropriate permission, letting a migration accurately reflect historical creation dates and original ownership rather than defaulting to migration-time values. A is incorrect: These fields are specifically designed to be settable via API during data migrations for authorized users. B is incorrect: Assigning everything to the admin permanently discards meaningful ownership data the business likely needs for reporting. D is incorrect: This capability is a native platform permission/API feature, not a separate paid product."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "During a migration, the consultant finds inconsistent field formats and missing required fields in the source data. What should the consultant do before proceeding?", options: [("A", "Load the data as-is and fix formatting issues afterward inside Salesforce"), ("B", "Skip all records with any missing field, regardless of business impact"), ("C", "Disable required field validation permanently to force the load through"), ("D", "Cleanse and standardize the source data (and back-fill or flag missing required values) before loading, rather than loading incomplete/inconsistent data into Salesforce")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Cleansing data before load prevents corrupt or incomplete records from ever entering the new system, which is far cheaper than remediating bad data after go-live. A is incorrect: Loading first and fixing later risks broken reports, automation failures, and user distrust in the interim. B is incorrect: Blanket-skipping every record with a missing field may discard legitimately valuable records that just need remediation, not deletion. C is incorrect: Permanently disabling required-field validation undermines data quality for all future records, not just the migration batch."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "A company wants to integrate its ERP with Salesforce to sync account and order data in near real time. What integration pattern should the consultant evaluate first?", options: [("A", "An event-driven / API-based integration (e.g., Platform Events or REST callouts) rather than a nightly batch file transfer"), ("B", "A quarterly manual CSV export/import"), ("C", "A one-time migration with no ongoing sync"), ("D", "Printing reports and re-keying data manually")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Near-real-time requirements point directly to an event-driven or API-based pattern that pushes/pulls changes as they occur, rather than a scheduled batch approach that introduces latency. B is incorrect: Quarterly manual exports are nowhere close to \"near real time\" and defeat the stated requirement. C is incorrect: A one-time migration doesn't address ongoing sync needs at all. D is incorrect: Manual re-keying is error-prone, slow, and the opposite of automated real-time integration."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "A company wants to integrate a marketing automation tool with Salesforce to sync leads/campaigns while minimizing duplicate data risk. What integration approach helps most?", options: [("A", "Insert new Lead records on every sync run regardless of existing matches"), ("B", "Use a native or AppExchange connector with matching-rule-aware upsert logic keyed on a stable external ID field"), ("C", "Disable duplicate rules entirely for the integration user"), ("D", "Keep native or AppExchange duplicate/matching rules active on the integration user so any records that slip past upsert logic are still flagged")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Upserting on a stable External ID lets the integration reliably match existing records instead of blindly inserting, directly minimizing duplicate creation during ongoing syncs. A is incorrect: Always inserting without matching guarantees duplicate Lead records accumulate over time. C is incorrect: Disabling duplicate rules for the integration user removes a safety net rather than minimizing duplicates."),
        Question(id: "101", question: "What are the implications of large data volumes (LDV) on sharing rule recalculation and report performance?", options: [("A", "LDV has no measurable impact on Salesforce performance"), ("B", "Sharing rules automatically scale infinitely with no configuration needed"), ("C", "Sharing recalculation jobs can take significantly longer and report queries may slow or time out without proper indexing, skinny tables, or selective filters"), ("D", "Reports become faster as data volume increases")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "At large scale, sharing recalculation and report queries become measurably slower without deliberate design choices (selective filters, custom indexes, skinny tables), which is a real architectural risk to flag proactively. A is incorrect: LDV is a well-documented Salesforce performance consideration with real, measurable effects. B is incorrect: Sharing recalculation performance is directly affected by data volume and hierarchy complexity; it doesn't scale automatically without design attention. D is incorrect: More data generally slows report execution, not speeds it up, absent optimization."),
        Question(id: "102", question: "A company plans to store 10 years of closed opportunity history and run reports across all years. What scalability implications should the consultant raise?", options: [("A", "Ten years of data has no effect on report performance"), ("B", "Salesforce automatically archives data older than one year with no configuration"), ("C", "Storage limits do not apply to Opportunity records"), ("D", "Report run times may degrade at scale, and the org may approach data storage limits, warranting archiving strategy discussions and selective, indexed report filters")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A decade of closed-opportunity history is a meaningful data volume that can affect both report performance and storage consumption, making an archiving conversation and indexed filtering a responsible proactive recommendation. A is incorrect: Report performance is directly influenced by the volume of data being queried and aggregated. B is incorrect: Salesforce does not auto-archive records; any archiving strategy must be deliberately designed and implemented. C is incorrect: Opportunity records consume data storage like any other object and are subject to org storage limits."),
        Question(id: "103", question: "A company has accounts with thousands of associated opportunities. What performance implications should the consultant consider for related lists and page load?", options: [("A", "Related list rendering can slow significantly at this scale, so the consultant should recommend filtered related lists, list view limits, or Lightning performance features to keep page load acceptable"), ("B", "Related lists have no row limits and always render instantly regardless of volume"), ("C", "The only fix is to delete older opportunities"), ("D", "Increase the org's API version to fix rendering speed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Thousands of child records on a related list is a known page-performance risk; filtering the related list or leveraging Lightning's optimized rendering (and pagination/limits) is the standard mitigation rather than accepting slow page loads. B is incorrect: Large related lists do measurably affect record page load times without mitigation. C is incorrect: Deleting historical opportunities destroys valuable data when filtering/pagination solves the performance problem without data loss. D is incorrect: API version governs integration behavior, not related-list UI rendering performance."),
        Question(id: "104", question: "When is it appropriate to recommend an external data archiving strategy rather than storing all historical records in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "Whenever a company has fewer than 100 total records"), ("B", "When historical data is rarely accessed operationally but must be retained for compliance/reporting, and keeping it in Salesforce would meaningfully strain storage costs or query performance"), ("C", "Archiving should never be considered under any circumstances"), ("D", "Only when the company switches away from Salesforce entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Archiving makes sense specifically when data's operational value has dropped (rarely queried day-to-day) but retention is still required, and keeping it live in Salesforce carries real storage/performance cost — a genuine cost/benefit trade-off. A is incorrect: Small record counts rarely justify the complexity of an archiving strategy at all. C is incorrect: Archiving is a standard, well-supported practice for managing storage and performance at scale. D is incorrect: Archiving decisions are relevant while actively using Salesforce, not only when leaving the platform."),
        Question(id: "105", question: "What is the purpose of matching rules, and how do they differ from duplicate rules?", options: [("A", "Matching rules and duplicate rules are the exact same configuration object"), ("B", "Duplicate rules define matching logic, while matching rules define the block/alert action"), ("C", "Matching rules define the logic used to identify potential duplicate records, while duplicate rules define what action to take (block, alert, report) when a match is found"), ("D", "Matching rules can be scoped to Account, Contact, Lead, or Person Account, not restricted to a single object")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Matching rules are the detection logic (how similarity is determined), while duplicate rules consume that logic to decide the resulting action — they're complementary but distinct components of the same feature. A is incorrect: They're separate, linked configuration objects with distinct responsibilities. B is incorrect: This reverses their actual roles; duplicate rules define the action, matching rules define the detection logic."),
        Question(id: "106", question: "What is the purpose of duplicate rules in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "To permanently delete duplicate records automatically without user input"), ("B", "To convert leads automatically when duplicates are found"), ("C", "To calculate currency conversion rates"), ("D", "To define the action taken (block creation, warn the user, or allow with a report) when a record matches an existing record per a linked matching rule")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Duplicate rules are the governance layer that decides how the system should respond (block/alert/report) once a matching rule flags a potential duplicate, giving admins control over the outcome. A is incorrect: Duplicate rules don't auto-delete; they alert or block, leaving the resolution decision to a user (or a separate merge action). B is incorrect: Duplicate detection is unrelated to lead conversion logic. C is incorrect: Currency conversion is handled by currency management features, unrelated to duplicate detection."),
        Question(id: "107", question: "How can validation rules and required fields enforce data quality without creating excessive friction?", options: [("A", "Apply required fields/validation rules narrowly to truly critical fields and stages, using conditional logic so rules only fire when relevant, rather than blanket-requiring every field everywhere"), ("B", "Make every field on every object required at all times"), ("C", "Validation rules should never include any conditional logic"), ("D", "Required fields cannot be applied per record type")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Scoping enforcement to genuinely critical data points, and using conditional logic so rules only apply in relevant contexts, balances data integrity against rep friction — rather than an all-or-nothing approach. B is incorrect: Making every field required everywhere creates excessive friction and encourages reps to enter junk data just to get past validation. C is incorrect: Conditional logic (e.g., only requiring a field at a certain stage) is exactly what keeps validation targeted and less disruptive. D is incorrect: Required fields can be scoped per record type via page layout-level field requiredness, giving flexibility across different processes."),
        Question(id: "108", question: "What are the trade-offs between enforcing data quality at entry time versus post-entry?", options: [("A", "There are no trade-offs; both approaches are functionally identical"), ("B", "Entry-time enforcement (validation rules, required fields) prevents bad data but can add friction; post-entry enforcement (dashboards, batch cleanup) is less intrusive but allows bad data to exist temporarily before correction"), ("C", "Post-entry enforcement is always superior in every scenario"), ("D", "Entry-time enforcement guarantees zero user friction")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The genuine trade-off is prevention-with-friction versus detection-with-lag: entry-time rules stop bad data immediately but can frustrate users, while post-entry monitoring is gentler but tolerates temporary data issues. A is incorrect: The two approaches have materially different timing and user-experience implications. C is incorrect: Post-entry approaches let inaccurate data persist (even briefly) in reports and processes, which isn't always acceptable. D is incorrect: Entry-time validation, by definition, can interrupt a user's workflow if their data doesn't meet criteria — that is friction."),
        Question(id: "109", question: "Opportunity records have inconsistent close dates and missing probability fields, making forecasting unreliable. What data quality measures should the consultant recommend?", options: [("A", "Disable forecasting entirely since the data can't be trusted"), ("B", "Ignore the issue since forecasting doesn't depend on these fields"), ("C", "Add validation rules requiring Close Date and Probability at key stages, and run a one-time cleanup batch/report to correct existing bad records"), ("D", "Delete all opportunities with missing fields")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Combining forward-looking validation (preventing new bad data) with a retroactive cleanup addresses both the ongoing cause and the existing backlog of inconsistent data undermining forecasts. A is incorrect: Disabling forecasting removes valuable pipeline visibility instead of fixing the underlying data problem. B is incorrect: Close Date and Probability directly drive forecast category rollups; they are core to forecast reliability. D is incorrect: Deleting affected records destroys potentially valuable pipeline data that could simply be corrected instead."),
        Question(id: "110", question: "A company wants to prevent inconsistent account name formats (e.g., \"Inc.\" vs. \"Incorporated\"). Which features help enforce data entry standards?", options: [("A", "Field-level security on the Account Name field"), ("B", "Duplicate Rules configured to alert or block on near-duplicate Account Name matches at the point of entry"), ("C", "Opportunity Splits"), ("D", "A validation rule restricting allowed suffix patterns, combined with matching/duplicate rules to catch near-duplicate variants that still slip through")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "A validation rule can enforce a standard suffix pattern at entry, while duplicate/matching rules catch variant spellings that still get through, together addressing both prevention and detection of formatting inconsistency. A is incorrect: Field-level security controls who can see/edit a field, not the format of its content. C is incorrect: Opportunity Splits allocate revenue credit among reps and has no bearing on account naming standards."),
        Question(id: "111", question: "A legacy CRM has a large volume of stale lead records. What approach should the consultant recommend for improving lead data quality before migration?", options: [("A", "Run a data quality audit to identify stale/invalid leads, apply criteria-based cleansing or archiving, and deduplicate before migrating only qualified, current records"), ("B", "Migrate every record regardless of age or validity to preserve complete history"), ("C", "Randomly sample 10% of leads and migrate only those"), ("D", "Skip data quality review since Salesforce will clean the data automatically post-migration")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Auditing and cleansing before migration ensures only valid, current, deduplicated leads enter the new org, avoiding importing years of stale, low-value records that would clutter reporting and pipelines. B is incorrect: Migrating everything regardless of validity imports the exact stale-data problem the company is trying to solve. C is incorrect: Random sampling has no relationship to data quality; it could just as easily exclude good records and include bad ones. D is incorrect: Salesforce provides no automatic post-migration data-cleaning mechanism; cleansing must be done deliberately."),
        Question(id: "112", question: "A company wants to measure and track data completeness scores across account and opportunity records over time. What approach should the consultant recommend?", options: [("A", "Manually review each record once per year in a spreadsheet"), ("B", "Build a formula field (or use a data quality tool) scoring completeness of key fields per record, then report/dashboard that score with historical trending via Historical Trend Reporting or scheduled snapshots"), ("C", "Data completeness cannot be measured within Salesforce"), ("D", "Rely solely on Einstein Opportunity Scoring for completeness tracking")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A formula-based completeness score, combined with trend reporting or periodic snapshots, gives a repeatable, quantifiable way to track data quality improvement over time directly within Salesforce. A is incorrect: Annual manual spreadsheet review doesn't scale and misses ongoing trend visibility. C is incorrect: Completeness scoring is achievable natively via formula fields and reporting; it isn't a Salesforce limitation. D is incorrect: Opportunity Scoring predicts win likelihood; it has no concept of field-level data completeness."),
        Question(id: "113", question: "What is the purpose of the Sales Call Summary feature in Agentforce for Sales, and what is required to enable it?", options: [("A", "It summarizes email threads only, never live calls"), ("B", "It requires no data or setup and works purely on Opportunity Amount"), ("C", "It generates an AI summary and follow-up actions from a recorded/transcribed sales call, requiring Conversation Insights/call recording capability to be enabled and configured"), ("D", "It replaces the need for any CRM record altogether")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Call summarization depends on having actual call audio/transcript data flowing in through a conversation-intelligence capability; without that source data enabled, there's nothing for the generative feature to summarize. A is incorrect: The feature is specifically built around call content, not email threads. B is incorrect: Meaningful summarization requires actual call transcript data as input; it can't be generated from Amount alone. D is incorrect: It enriches the Opportunity/Activity record; it doesn't eliminate the need for CRM records."),
        Question(id: "114", question: "What generative AI capabilities does Agentforce for Sales provide for opportunity briefing before customer meetings?", options: [("A", "Automatic creation of a new Account record before every meeting"), ("B", "Currency conversion for the opportunity amount"), ("C", "Deletion of stale activity records"), ("D", "An AI-generated briefing summarizing opportunity history, recent activity, and key contacts/context ahead of an upcoming meeting")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Meeting-prep briefings are a generative use case that synthesize existing opportunity/activity/contact data into a concise pre-meeting summary, saving the rep manual review time. A is incorrect: Briefing generation reads existing records; it doesn't create new Account records. B is incorrect: Currency conversion is a data configuration feature unrelated to AI briefing generation. C is incorrect: Briefing generation summarizes data; it has no data-deletion function."),
        Question(id: "115", question: "A company wants AI to draft a follow-up email referencing key discussion points and proposed next steps after a sales call. Which feature addresses this?", options: [("A", "Generative email drafting within Agentforce for Sales, grounded in the call summary/transcript data"), ("B", "Web-to-Lead"), ("C", "Territory Management"), ("D", "Advanced Currency Management")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Generative email drafting is designed to take call context (summary, discussion points) and produce a relevant, ready-to-edit follow-up email, directly matching this use case. B is incorrect: Web-to-Lead only handles inbound lead capture, unrelated to outbound email drafting. C is incorrect: Territory Management governs assignment structure, not content generation. D is incorrect: Currency management has no relevance to email drafting."),
        Question(id: "116", question: "A company wants reps to auto-generate a personalized email draft based on the Opportunity record and recent activity. Which feature supports this?", options: [("A", "Duplicate Rules"), ("B", "Einstein/Agentforce generative email assistance grounded in the Opportunity's record data and activity history"), ("C", "Enterprise Territory Management"), ("D", "Opportunity Splits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "This generative capability is specifically built to pull context from the Opportunity record and its activity history to produce a personalized draft, rather than a generic template. A is incorrect: Duplicate Rules manage record matching; irrelevant to drafting content. C is incorrect: Territory Management governs assignment/visibility, not content generation. D is incorrect: Opportunity Splits allocate credit among team members, unrelated to email drafting."),
        Question(id: "117", question: "A company wants an AI agent to autonomously follow up with prospects after a demo, send tailored content, and schedule next steps. Which capability supports this?", options: [("A", "A static email template with no personalization"), ("B", "Territory-based Forecasting"), ("C", "An Agentforce autonomous sales agent configured with defined actions (send content, schedule meeting) and guardrails for post-demo follow-up"), ("D", "Path field-set guidance")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "This describes autonomous, multi-step agent behavior (not just a single generative draft) — exactly what Agentforce agents are built for, executing defined actions within configured guardrails without a human triggering each step. A is incorrect: A static template has no personalization or autonomous action-taking capability. B is incorrect: Territory-based Forecasting aggregates pipeline value; it has no agentic follow-up capability. D is incorrect: Path guidance is UI-level guidance for a human rep, not autonomous execution."),
        Question(id: "118", question: "What is Einstein Lead Scoring, how does it work, and what data does it use?", options: [("A", "It assigns scores based solely on the alphabetical order of the lead's last name"), ("B", "It requires no historical data and works immediately in a brand-new org"), ("C", "It only scores Leads that have already converted"), ("D", "It analyzes historical lead conversion data and lead attributes to assign a numeric score indicating likelihood to convert")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Lead Scoring is a predictive model trained on historical conversion outcomes and lead attributes, producing a score that reflects statistically learned conversion likelihood. A is incorrect: Scoring is data-driven and predictive, not based on arbitrary attributes like name ordering. B is incorrect: Predictive models need sufficient historical data to train meaningfully; a brand-new org with no history won't produce reliable scores immediately. C is incorrect: Scoring applies to open, unconverted leads to predict future conversion likelihood, not already-converted ones."),
        Question(id: "119", question: "A sales manager wants the CRM to score each open opportunity by likelihood to close. Which feature provides this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Opportunity Scoring"), ("B", "Einstein Lead Scoring"), ("C", "Campaign Influence"), ("D", "Advanced Currency Management")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Opportunity Scoring is specifically the predictive model trained to evaluate open deals and output a likelihood-to-close score, matching this exact requirement. B is incorrect: Lead Scoring applies to Leads, not open Opportunities. C is incorrect: Campaign Influence attributes marketing touches to revenue; it isn't a close-likelihood predictor. D is incorrect: Currency management is unrelated to predictive scoring."),
        Question(id: "120", question: "What is the difference between Einstein Opportunity Scoring and Einstein Forecasting?", options: [("A", "They are the exact same feature under two names"), ("B", "Opportunity Scoring predicts an individual deal's likelihood to close, while Einstein Forecasting predicts aggregate future pipeline/revenue trends at a team or org level"), ("C", "Opportunity Scoring aggregates team-level revenue; Forecasting scores individual deals"), ("D", "Neither feature uses historical data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The scope differs meaningfully: Scoring operates at the individual-record level (this deal's odds), while Forecasting operates at an aggregate level (predicted total revenue across a team/period) — different granularity, different use case. A is incorrect: They serve distinct purposes at different levels of granularity, not interchangeable names for one feature. C is incorrect: This reverses their actual scopes; Scoring is deal-level, Forecasting is aggregate-level. D is incorrect: Both are predictive/statistical features that rely on historical data patterns to generate their outputs."),
        Question(id: "121", question: "When should a consultant recommend Einstein Opportunity Scoring versus Einstein Forecasting?", options: [("A", "Always recommend both regardless of the actual business need"), ("B", "Opportunity Scoring is only for quota-setting; Forecasting is only for individual deal coaching"), ("C", "Opportunity Scoring when reps need deal-level prioritization guidance; Forecasting when leadership needs aggregate revenue projection for planning"), ("D", "Neither feature is appropriate for any Sales Cloud use case")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Matching the feature to the actual consumer of the insight — reps needing deal prioritization vs. leadership needing aggregate projections — is the practical basis for the recommendation. A is incorrect: Recommending both indiscriminately ignores whether the actual need is deal-level or aggregate-level insight. B is incorrect: This reverses their actual use cases; Scoring is deal-level (not quota-setting), Forecasting is aggregate (not individual coaching). D is incorrect: Both are established, appropriate Sales Cloud features for their respective use cases."),
        Question(id: "122", question: "A company wants to surface the next-best action for each rep based on deal stage, activity, and buyer engagement. Which feature provides this?", options: [("A", "Web-to-Lead"), ("B", "Duplicate Management"), ("C", "Campaign Hierarchies"), ("D", "Einstein/Agentforce Recommendations surfaced contextually on the Opportunity record")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "This recommendation engine is designed to synthesize deal stage, activity, and engagement signals into an actionable next-step suggestion directly where the rep is working. A is incorrect: Web-to-Lead captures inbound form submissions; unrelated to in-deal recommendations. B is incorrect: Duplicate Management prevents redundant records; no recommendation function. C is incorrect: Campaign Hierarchies organize marketing programs, not deal-level next actions."),
        Question(id: "123", question: "A company wants to identify which accounts are likely to churn or expand based on engagement/usage signals. Which Einstein feature is most relevant?", options: [("A", "Einstein/Agentforce predictive account health or engagement scoring built on usage and engagement data"), ("B", "Einstein Lead Scoring"), ("C", "Advanced Currency Management"), ("D", "Path")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Predictive account-health scoring is the capability designed specifically to model churn/expansion risk from ongoing engagement and usage signals, distinct from lead-conversion prediction. B is incorrect: Lead Scoring predicts lead conversion likelihood, not existing-account churn/expansion risk. C is incorrect: Currency management is unrelated to account health prediction. D is incorrect: Path is UI stage guidance, with no predictive modeling capability."),
        Question(id: "124", question: "What are the data volume and quality prerequisites for enabling Einstein predictive features in Sales Cloud?", options: [("A", "No historical data is required; the model works from day one with zero records"), ("B", "A sufficient volume of historical, consistently populated records (e.g., closed opportunities with accurate outcomes) is required for the underlying model to train reliably"), ("C", "Only the Account object's data matters; Opportunity data quality is irrelevant"), ("D", "Predictive features require disabling all validation rules")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Predictive models need enough consistent historical outcome data to learn meaningful patterns; sparse or low-quality historical data directly undermines score reliability — a genuine prerequisite to communicate to stakeholders. A is incorrect: Without historical data, there's nothing for the model to learn from, so predictions would be unreliable or unavailable. C is incorrect: Opportunity Scoring depends heavily on Opportunity-level historical outcomes, not just Account data. D is incorrect: Validation rules support data quality, which actually helps predictive features; disabling them would hurt data quality, not help scoring."),
        Question(id: "125", question: "A company wants Agentforce to autonomously qualify inbound leads by asking clarifying questions before routing to a rep. What capability best matches this?", options: [("A", "A static Web-to-Lead form with no follow-up logic"), ("B", "Territory-based Forecasting"), ("C", "An Agentforce agent configured with a qualification topic/action flow that interacts with the lead and routes based on defined criteria"), ("D", "Advanced Currency Management")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "This is an autonomous, multi-turn agent use case — asking clarifying questions and acting on the responses — which is exactly what a configured Agentforce agent with defined topics/actions is designed to execute. A is incorrect: A static form captures initial input only; it can't ask follow-up clarifying questions or make routing decisions. B is incorrect: Forecasting aggregates pipeline value; unrelated to lead qualification conversations. D is incorrect: Currency management has no bearing on lead qualification logic."),
        Question(id: "126", question: "A company wants to use generative AI to draft a personalized outreach email for a specific prospect segment while keeping brand voice consistent. What should the consultant recommend?", options: [("A", "Let the AI send emails automatically with no human review or brand guidance"), ("B", "Disable all personalization to keep messaging generic"), ("C", "Use only Web-to-Lead auto-response emails for all outreach"), ("D", "Use Prompt Templates (grounded in merge fields/record data) with brand-voice guidelines built into the prompt, reviewed before sending")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Prompt Templates let the consultant embed both data grounding (so content is relevant) and brand-voice instructions directly into the generation logic, with a human-review step preserving quality control before sending. A is incorrect: Sending with zero human review or brand guidance risks off-brand or inaccurate messaging reaching prospects. B is incorrect: Disabling personalization defeats the stated goal of personalized outreach entirely. C is incorrect: Web-to-Lead auto-responses are generic transactional acknowledgments, not personalized segment-targeted outreach."),
        Question(id: "127", question: "A sales leader wants to understand which specific factors are driving an opportunity's Einstein Score up or down, to coach a rep effectively. What should the consultant point them to?", options: [("A", "The score's contributing-factors breakdown (top positive/negative indicators) provided alongside the numeric score"), ("B", "The Opportunity Amount field alone"), ("C", "The Lead Source field on the original converted lead"), ("D", "There is no way to see what drives an Einstein Score")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Einstein Scoring surfaces explainability factors (the specific signals pushing the score up or down) precisely so managers can coach reps on the underlying drivers, not just the resulting number. B is incorrect: Amount is one static field; it doesn't reveal the multiple behavioral/activity factors influencing the score. C is incorrect: Lead Source reflects the original lead's origin, not the current deal's scoring factors. D is incorrect: Explainability factors are a designed part of the scoring feature's output, not something hidden or absent."),
        Question(id: "128", question: "A company wants to ensure Agentforce agents deployed for sales use cases operate within defined boundaries (e.g., not offering unapproved discounts). What should the consultant configure?", options: [("A", "Guardrails/topic-scoped actions and approval limits within the agent's configuration to constrain what actions it can autonomously take"), ("B", "No configuration is needed; agents self-limit automatically with no setup"), ("C", "Disable the agent's access to any CRM data"), ("D", "Only use agents for internal HR questions, never sales-related actions")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Agentforce is designed with configurable guardrails and scoped actions specifically so an org can constrain autonomous behavior (e.g., discount limits) to defined, approved boundaries. B is incorrect: Guardrails require deliberate configuration; agents don't self-limit without setup. C is incorrect: Disabling CRM data access would cripple the agent's usefulness for sales entirely, an overcorrection instead of proper scoping. D is incorrect: Restricting agents to HR-only use ignores the legitimate, guardrailed sales use cases Agentforce supports."),
        Question(id: "129", question: "A company wants to compare the ROI of deploying an autonomous Agentforce sales agent versus a traditional Sales Engagement cadence for SDR outreach. What should the consultant highlight as the key differentiator?", options: [("A", "Cadences follow a pre-defined, human-authored sequence, while an Agentforce agent can dynamically adapt its next action based on real-time prospect responses and signals"), ("B", "Both approaches are functionally and technically identical"), ("C", "Cadences require no configuration at all, while agents require none either"), ("D", "Agentforce agents cannot send emails under any circumstance")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The core differentiator is adaptability: a cadence executes a fixed sequence regardless of response, while an agent can reason over incoming signals and adjust its next action dynamically — a meaningful capability difference for ROI discussions. B is incorrect: Their underlying mechanisms (fixed sequence vs. adaptive reasoning) are fundamentally different. C is incorrect: Both require real configuration — cadence steps and templates for one, topics/actions/guardrails for the other. D is incorrect: Agentforce agents can be configured to send emails as one of their available actions."),
        Question(id: "130", question: "A company wants an AI feature to predict which deals are at risk of slipping to next quarter based on engagement and activity patterns. Which capability addresses this?", options: [("A", "Einstein Opportunity Scoring combined with activity-recency signals, surfaced as a risk indicator on at-risk deals"), ("B", "Advanced Currency Management"), ("C", "Campaign Hierarchies"), ("D", "Duplicate Rules")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Combining a declining or low likelihood-to-close score with stagnant activity signals is exactly the predictive-plus-behavioral combination that flags deals at risk of slipping, giving reps and managers early warning. B is incorrect: Currency management is a data configuration feature with no predictive risk capability. C is incorrect: Campaign Hierarchies organize marketing programs, unrelated to deal-slip prediction. D is incorrect: Duplicate Rules manage record matching, with no bearing on deal-risk prediction.")
    ]
}
